#
# Windows (all builds): build the ActiveQt idc tool with the target toolchain.
#
# idc.pro declares option(host_build), which makes qmake build it with the host
# spec - linux-g++ here - while its main.cpp includes qt_windows.h. No Linux host
# has windows.h, so the module cannot build at all in a cross compile:
#
#   qt_windows.h:64:10: fatal error: windows.h: No such file or directory
#
# idc is a target-side tool despite that declaration: it edits type libraries
# into Windows binaries, so it is only ever run on Windows. Dropping the one line
# builds it with the target toolchain, and bin/idc.exe comes out a target PE.
#
# Nothing about it is XP-specific, as the diagnosis above shows: every Windows
# target needs it, and with it all three carry the identical module set. A
# module missing from one target quietly weakens every coverage comparison
# against it.
#
# Changes:
#   qtactiveqt/src/tools/idc/idc.pro
#       build idc with the target toolchain instead of the host one
#
# Apply: git apply, from the Qt source root (qt-everywhere-src-5.15.19/)
diff --git a/qtactiveqt/src/tools/idc/idc.pro b/qtactiveqt/src/tools/idc/idc.pro
index 7f9a8d45f..6f3548a5d 100644
--- a/qtactiveqt/src/tools/idc/idc.pro
+++ b/qtactiveqt/src/tools/idc/idc.pro
@@ -1,4 +1,3 @@
-option(host_build)
 CONFIG += force_bootstrap
 SOURCES = main.cpp
 
